  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why don't penguins' feet freeze?",
      "a": "In a penguin's legs, warm blood going down to the feet passes close to cold blood coming back and warms it. The feet are kept just above freezing, cold enough to lose little heat but warm enough not to freeze.",
      "asks": [
        "How do penguins stand on ice?",
        "What is countercurrent heat exchange?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does fur keep animals warm?",
      "a": "Fur and feathers trap a layer of still air next to the skin. Still air is a poor conductor of heat, so body heat escapes slowly. Animals fluff up their fur or feathers to trap even more air when it is very cold.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are polar bears so furry?",
        "Why does trapped air keep animals warm?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do animals make extra heat?",
      "a": "They shiver, which makes muscles release heat, and many mammals burn a special brown fat that turns stored energy straight into heat. Eating more food fuels both.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do animals shiver?",
        "What is brown fat?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is blubber?",
      "a": "Blubber is a thick layer of fat under the skin of seals, whales and walruses. It insulates them in icy water, where fur works less well, and also stores energy and helps them float.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do whales have blubber?",
        "How thick is seal blubber?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do small birds survive freezing nights?",
      "a": "Small birds like chickadees fluff their feathers, shiver all night and may let their body temperature drop several degrees to save energy. Many roost together in tree holes and must eat a lot each day to rebuild their fat.",
      "asks": [
        "How do tiny birds stay warm in winter?",
        "Why do birds fluff up in the cold?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "An arctic fox in its winter coat does not need to raise its heat production until about minus 40 °C (Scholander and colleagues, 1950).",
    "Emperor penguins huddle in groups of hundreds or thousands, slowly shuffling so birds take turns in the warm centre."
  ],
